<metadata>
    <!--
        The element name is the widget id, and core uses it as the key of this widget in every
        user's saved dashboard layout. Renaming it would silently drop the widget off the
        dashboards it is already on, so it stays "fanctl".

        Every string in <translations> is passed through gettext() by the dashboard controller, so
        the English text here is the msgid the plugin's i18n catalogues translate. Nothing the
        widget prints is written in the JavaScript.

        NOTHING inside <translations>, including a comment. The controller does

            $translations = (array)($metadataAttributes->translations ?? []);
            foreach ($translations as $key => $value) { $translations[$key] = gettext($value); }

        (V: Core/Api/DashboardController.php:124,136 on OPNsense 26.7.4_1) and an XML comment in
        there arrives as a "comment" key like any other child. One comment is a SimpleXMLElement,
        which gettext() coerces through __toString() and nobody notices. Two or more are an array,
        and gettext() raises a TypeError that is caught and turned into

            {"errorMessage":"Unexpected error, check log for details"}

        from get_dashboard - which is the API the whole dashboard is built from, so EVERY widget
        disappears and the Lobby shows one red box. Measured here: three comments in this element
        emptied the dashboard, and moving them out brought it back.

        So the notes that belong to those strings are written here, above the element, where they
        cost nothing. Nobody should have to learn this the way it was learned.

        ==== the strings ======================================================================

        mode_*      What the plugin is doing, in one line above the bars. "monitor" is the default
                    and it is not a failure: it means the plugin is reading and writing nothing,
                    which is what it does until somebody has measured a floor in front of the
                    machine.

        incident    The one red thing this widget has. It says what to do rather than what
                    happened, because a restore that failed is the single state in this plugin
                    that a person has to act on, and a warm reboot does not necessarily clear a
                    fan register.

        state_*     A channel reading zero is not a stopped fan. It may be a header with no fan on
                    it, or a fan whose tachometer is not wired to this chip, and nothing in
                    software can tell those apart - so the word "stopped" is never used about one.

        cores       The processor's cores as one line, the hottest of them, with their count: on
                    these machines every core reads the same, and one bar per core made this the
                    tallest widget on the dashboard.
    -->
    <fanctl>
        <filename>FanCtl.js</filename>
        <link>/ui/fanctl</link>
        <endpoints>
            <endpoint>/api/fanctl/service/*</endpoint>
        </endpoints>
        <translations>
            <title>Fan Control</title>
            <mode_monitor>reading only - no fan is being driven</mode_monitor>
            <mode_curve>following the curve</mode_curve>
            <mode_manual>held at a fixed speed</mode_manual>
            <not_running>Fan control is not running. These readings were taken just now.</not_running>
            <disabled>Fan control is turned off.</disabled>
            <incident>The fans could not be handed back. Power the appliance off and on - a restart may not clear it.</incident>
            <state_turning>turning</state_turning>
            <state_driven>driven by this plugin</state_driven>
            <state_silent>no tachometer signal</state_silent>
            <silent_summary>%s channels report no tachometer signal</silent_summary>
            <unit_rpm>rpm</unit_rpm>
            <over_limit>At or above the limit, where the fans go to full speed</over_limit>
            <over_panic>Above the panic point - the plugin hands the fans back here</over_panic>
            <scale>Scale ends at the panic point, %s; the mark is the limit, %s</scale>
            <cores>processor cores (%s)</cores>
            <nodata>No fan or temperature readings yet.</nodata>
            <stale>The readings are from %s and are no longer being updated.</stale>
            <unreachable>Could not read the fan control status.</unreachable>
        </translations>
    </fanctl>
</metadata>
